WEBVTT

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:14.180
My fellow Americans, we have endured a week such as no nation should live through.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:20.070
The number of 1967 was really a crucible.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:29.820
You see in city after city these uprisings in a broad enough way that it can't be simply dismissed as coincidence.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:37.900
Conditions were such in every one of these cities that almost any spark could set them off.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.030
You had Newark, you had Detroit, you had cities all across the country up in flames.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.030
We're wondering, can we actually have a country?

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.110
I'm tonight appointing a special advisory commission on civil disorders.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.490
The commission will investigate the origins of the recent disorders in our cities.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:06.240
It will make recommendations to me, to the Congress, to the state governors, and to the mayors,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.920
for measures prevent or contain such disasters in the future.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.860
He said, answer three questions.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.680
What happened? Why did it happen?

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.550
What can be done to prevent it from happening again and again?

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.660
The commission held 20 days of hearings.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.860
We heard from a lot of witnesses.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.180
They really were trying to get a baseline of knowledge about why American cities were on fire.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:43.220
We had members of the President's Commission on Civil Disorders here in our city today.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:50.840
The Kerner Commission avoided this tendency to listen only to certain sections of society.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:58.820
We can learn a good deal more out over the country talking to people than we can sitting in a building in Washington.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.920
I trust that the report will be completely honest.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.260
That being the case, it will describe undoubtedly a very serious condition in the country.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.280
The commission told the truth, but it was a harsh truth.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.260
Why are you put on the riot commission?

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.720
On the riot commission.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.730
Throughout the neighborhood, more than 2,000 copies of the riot report were sold in less than a week.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.340
It really becomes the centerpiece of conversation.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.640
You see civic groups, churches, libraries.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.740
Even police departments buying the book in bulk.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.400
These are the major proposals.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.760
A form of guaranteed annual income.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.600
Six million new low and middle income units.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.630
Two million new jobs in government and private business.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.910
The cost has been estimated at two billion dollars a month.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.470
Something like the cost of the war in Vietnam.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.820
Once it became clear what a political bombshell this might represent,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.900
Lyndon Johnson wants to take the legs out from under him.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.180
Precinct, you're down around Broad Street.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.940
Bam, it's supposed to be planned.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:51.860
Precinct, car.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:03:59.090
Precinct.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:14.600
Ladies and gentlemen, ladies and gentlemen, let me have your attention, please.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:23.380
Governor Hughes and the New York City officials have earlier issued a proclamation.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:29.970
There will be a curfew upon pedestrians starting at...

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:55.780
The city of those sections is apparently still in what can only be classified as open rebellion.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.980
And Mayor Adnesio and I have determined that the line...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.580
between jungle assault on law and order might as well be drawn here as any place in america

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:23.440
richard hughes says the situation in newark is under control with the exception of sniper fire

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.340
and he says there is no way to control sniper fire

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.860
two blocks of newark springfield avenue here in the ghetto area and this friday they all look

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:57.820
pretty much like this one massive sea of devil's station as far as the eye can see in directions

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.750
broken glass, all that's left after a night of violent rioting.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:05.820
How much did you lose?

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.940
About $15,000 to $20,000.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:09.410
But it isn't that.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.970
It's all my life that I've put in here for 18 years.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.170
They built up a little business and now I'm forced out.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:14.590
Why?

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.810
Because he's professional, no-goodniks, bums, and what have you.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.480
Listen, all we want is an equal right.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.740
That's all we're asking for.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.040
And as long as they keep this up, we're going to burn the white out.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:48.100
The civil disorders, violent protests that erupted in so many of America's cities during the hot summer of 1967 were just sensational front page, big type news.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:59.270
And the same was true on television. They just dominated the television news. The fires, the looting, sniping.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:28.130
yesterday was on the west side of the city but it's spread pretty well across the city now so

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.890
you're dealing with what is 139

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.260
square miles or something like that.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:42.300
As governor of the state of Michigan I do hereby officially request the immediate

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:48.680
deployment of into Michigan is in re-establishing law in the city.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:59.000
It caused enormous fear and anger, puzzlement.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:09.800
43 Americans died in these streets, 386 were injured, 477 buildings were damaged or destroyed.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.800
Something like it happened in 76 American cities this summer.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:24.700
The question was, is this some kind of organized thing?

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.140
How long is it going to last?

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.670
What can be done to stop it?

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.400
Next summer, it could look like this in the downtown sections of our cities.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:42.290
It could look like this in the neighborhood where you live.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:46.740
Simple fact is this.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:50.640
We are in the worst crisis we have known since the Civil War.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:55.500
Since Newark and Detroit, law and order have become obsessions on Capitol Hill,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.980
and the main direction of congressional movement is toward investigating committees and...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.880
anti-riot bills, gun control legislation, more police support.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:11.570
At the height of the Detroit riots, I introduced a resolution in the Senate.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:18.080
I said, we ought to have a blue-ribbon citizens commission to look into these civil disorders,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.640
not just on a law and order basis, but getting at deeper causes.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.890
That was on July the 25th of 1967.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:37.360
On the 27th, it was announced by the White House that President Johnson was going to have a nationwide telecast.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:43.040
And he called me that evening, not very much before he was to go on the air.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:50.820
And he said to me, Fred, I'm going to appoint that commission you've been talking about, and I'm going to put you on it.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.340
And I said, well, I'll do the best I can.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:59.830
And he said, another thing, Fred, I want you to remember you're a Johnson man.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.130
And I said, yes, sir, I am a Johnson man.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:07.810
And he said, if you forget it, I'll take my pocket knife and cut your blank off.

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.620
He did not say blank.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:17.440
My fellow Americans, we have endured a week such as no nation should live through,

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.020
a time of violence and tragedy.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:26.110
For a few minutes tonight, I want to talk about that tragedy.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.670
And I want to talk about the deeper questions that it raises.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:31.100
for us all.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:35.730
I am tonight appointing a special advisory commission on civil disorders.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:42.170
I think he just wanted to be sure that he would get all the credit for what he had been

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.770
doing in regard to race and poverty.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:50.420
And I should say that Lyndon Johnson did more against racism and against poverty than any

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:56.520
president before or since, with the sole exception of probably Abraham Lincoln.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:09.600
It would be fatal for the nation to overlook the urgency of the moment.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:17.560
This sweltering summit of the Negroes' legitimate discontent passed

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:22.510
until that is an invigorating altar of freedom and equality.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.730
They called it the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.760
More than 200,000 of them came to Washington this morning

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.240
in a kind of climax to a historic spring and summer

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.980
in the struggle for equal rights.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.440
1963 is not an end, but a beginning.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:50.800
Those who hoped to grow needed to blow off steam

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:53.760
and will now be content

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.580
will have a rude evening

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.340
if the nation returns to business as usual.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:12.160
Johnson took up the mantle of John Kennedy.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.680
President Kennedy had recommended civil rights legislation

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.040
but had no chance to pass it.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:29.970
I've talked long enough in this country about equal rights.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:42.670
rights. We have talked for a hundred years or more. It is time now to write the next chapter

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:53.890
and to write it in the books of law. Johnson made that really a major thing and I think felt

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:59.960
very deeply about it. Lyndon Baines Johnson and the Great Society. Two weeks before he took

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.160
the oath of office for the first term as president in his own right, Mr. Johnson spelled out to

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:13.750
Congress what he wanted for the great society. First, to keep our economy growing, to open for

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:24.230
all Americans the opportunity that is now enjoyed by most Americans, and to improve the quality of

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.470
life for all.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.640
In the first two or three years of Johnson's administration,

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.400
owing to large Democratic majorities in Congress

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:40.120
and a well of goodwill following the Kennedy assassination,

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.560
Johnson was able to pass a sweeping domestic agenda.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:46.780
Civil Rights Act of 1964,

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:49.970
which desegregated all public accommodations,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.300
passed the Voting Rights Act of 1965,

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.040
which essentially desegregated the voting process

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:59.920
throughout the entire U.S. South.

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:11.260
millions of americans are denied the right to vote because of their color today is a triumph freedom

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:22.460
as huge as any victory that's ever been won on any battlefield today the negro story

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:28.150
and the American story and blend.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:50.000
Johnson also initiated the war on poverty and authorizes nearly a billion dollars to specifically target the places and institutions where poverty is created and recreated in education, in employment.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:59.860
It is an actual good faith effort to move the needle some on what American poverty looks like, particularly in disenfranchised communities.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.230
It is not enough just to open the gates of opportunity.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:14.450
All our citizens must have the ability to walk through those gates.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:23.390
This is the next and the more profound stage of the battle for civil rights.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:29.870
Lyndon Johnson had made the calculation about what investment in civil rights

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.120
would mean politically.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:37.460
He is aware that every significant step he takes

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:40.810
simultaneously feeds this growing backlash,

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:42.890
or what people have called whitelash,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:44.640
to the civil rights movement.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:49.400
The idea that Johnson had set in motion

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.500
a kind of government overreach.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.300
You saw that manifest itself as early as 1964

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:59.500
when Barry Goldwater entered the presidential race.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.940
challenged Johnson. It was very clear that he was tapping into a reserve of white anger, a belief

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:10.490
that somehow the Great Society and Johnson's civil rights agenda were going to be threatening

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:15.430
to their communities, to their jobs, and to their safety. While the president of the United States

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:22.910
speaks of the Great Society, our cities and suburbs are turning into the lawless society.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:29.960
Old Water is making the argument that the...

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:34.940
The doctrine of non-violent civil disobedience that is promoted by Dr. King and other civil

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:41.310
rights leaders is fundamentally encouraging a disrespect for authority and for law.

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:46.400
This is a claim that had been articulated before.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:53.020
Southern segregationists are famous in the congressional record and on the floor of Congress

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:57.700
for saying that civil rights agitation is breeding crime.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:16:59.980
so you've

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.980
got, on the one hand, one of the largest political struggles for racial justice in our nation's

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:13.820
history happening. And at that time, legislators are actively arguing that we ought to treat

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:21.980
protesters as ordinary thugs. Nothing prepares the way for tyranny more than the failure of

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:28.620
public officials to keep the streets safe from bullies and marauders. He's making the argument

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:29.980
that not only has the

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.800
federal government expanded too much. Not only is it spending too many tax dollars on too wide a

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:42.960
range of social programs, but those social programs themselves are fueling the troubles that are

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:56.480
beginning to appear in America's cities. In the 1960s, the U.S. begins to witness massive

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.980
demographic changes that resulted from the Great...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.280
migration of rural black Americans in the southern states to northern and western cities.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:14.280
Cities were becoming blacker. Washington, D.C. had come into a black majority by 1960.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:21.500
Detroit and Cleveland, black people were edging majorities. This was alarming to federal

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:27.660
policymakers because as black populations and cities are growing, there are fewer and fewer

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.980
we're jobs. Deindustrialization is our

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.180
happening there's a sense that society is breaking down and it was breaking down

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:43.530
black people were saying like enough social scientists and policymakers in

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:50.450
the early 1960s start to label black youth as social dynamite given this

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:56.050
cocktail of population change and continued inequality this group of

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:58.850
social dynamite these youth of color would explode

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:04.470
And that's exactly what happened.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.270
There's mooting at this moment of 88th and Broadway.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:10.610
At 103rd and Compton, a Molotov cocktail

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:11.850
was thrown a short time ago.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.330
And in that area are about 3,000 or 4,000 rioters.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:21.480
A bloody rampage in a crowded Negro neighborhood called Watts.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:25.710
Incredible scene.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.770
Gun battle in the middle of Broadway.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.070
in the streets of Los Angeles.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:41.320
A small army of policemen, most of them,

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.240
carrying shotguns, National Guardsmen

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:44.760
with .30 caliber machine guns.

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.210
Bodies of several Negroes who have been shot

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.250
already in this battle.

00:19:58.010 --> 00:19:59.870
Johnson is at his ranch in Texas.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.400
and Joe Califano, his domestic policy chief,

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:05.900
spent two days trying to get the president on the phone

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:06.440
and couldn't.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:07.800
Johnson simply shut down.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:14.460
This is just days after he's cashed in all of his political chips

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.280
to shepherd the Voting Rights Act through Congress.

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:19.870
It's a kind of indignation,

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:22.610
almost a sense of being slapped in the face.

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.760
We just had the most massive legislation in U.S. history,

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.240
certainly since the Civil War,

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:29.960
extending the rights to...

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:32.290
African-Americans, why aren't you happy?

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:37.910
No reason at all.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.110
Bottles and rocks and broken windows.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.820
Almost a mad holiday atmosphere.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:47.620
People laughing, cheering on those who find things to take home.

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:52.160
Stores broken into the one directly across the street, a pawn shop.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.500
These reporters might as well have been foreign correspondents.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:56.740
Can I talk with you for just a moment?

00:20:58.160 --> 00:20:58.860
Overwhelmingly right.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.120
Overwhelmingly male, overwhelmingly middle, upper class.

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.070
They were covering a different nation.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:10.500
The burning and looting, the shooting and beating went on for nearly a week.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:15.060
In the background is a long chronicle of defeat and disappointment,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:20.140
of discrimination and Negro grievances, of pure hate for the white man.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.870
The news coverage was lurid.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:29.980
The tone was big cities in America are...

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:39.350
exploding and we should all be afraid for johnson there's a real fear that this violence is somehow

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:46.600
going to undercut the war on poverty he's put in this position where he's got to maintain his

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:53.560
commitment to addressing racial discrimination but also his commitment to restore law and order

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:59.880
a rider with a molotov cocktail in his hands is not

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.530
fighting for civil rights any more than a Klansman with a sheet on his back and a mask

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:16.460
on his face. They are both more or less what the law declares them. Lawbreakers, destroyers of

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:22.670
constitutional rights and liberties, and would ultimately destroy a free America.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:29.970
On the heels of Watts, Congress passes by unanimous vote the law enforcement.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:37.020
Assistance Act. And that's really the point at which we can mark the beginnings of the war on

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:47.470
crime. In theory, this war on crime coexists with this war on poverty, almost as a kind of stick

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:55.400
and carrot. And it funnels millions of dollars into increasing numbers of police and experimental

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:59.980
crime control projects that were being enacted by and large.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.400
in low-income communities of color.

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:07.340
This is the first moment that we see the federal government

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:10.980
actually sending money to help support crime control.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.650
Crime is no longer a state and local issue.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:16.850
The federal government's going to get involved.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:21.790
It's setting up a precedent for evermore investment.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:27.950
But the war crime in no way quelled the uprisings.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:29.650
You have even more.

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:32.330
of those kind of conflicts in 1966,

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:36.950
uprisings in 38 American cities,

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:40.460
from San Francisco to Providence, Rhode Island.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.160
I think America must see

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:48.700
that riots do not develop out of thin air.

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:54.160
Certain conditions continue to exist in our society,

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:58.130
which must be condemned as vigorously

00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:59.980
as we condemn riots.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:19.420
The image of civil rights in many white Americans' minds had been of African Americans beseeching the nation for decent treatment, for equality, asking white America to just accord them their basic humanity.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:29.680
The images of well-dressed sit-in demonstrators being beaten, being spat upon are so important to understanding white Americans' sympathetic attitudes.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:38.010
towards civil rights, and then you see a fundamental transition where media simply

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:44.810
can't resist the spectacle of urban disorder. The camera simply cannot look

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:51.070
away, and as much as it unsettles white viewers, there's also this sort of

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:56.040
fixation. In some ways it's confirming their darkest prejudices about black

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.980
Americans. Now they're seeing a more

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.340
menacing face of black America, and they emphatically do not like what they're seeing.

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:12.150
What you start to see is this phrase, we're rewarding the rioters.

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:22.200
We're creating a climate where people understand that through uprisings, through rioting, through

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:28.200
looting, they can gain the support of liberals who will then shower communities with new

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:29.600
and expensive social programs.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:38.110
subsidized by angry and anxious white voters outside of urban america lewis harris is one of

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:43.830
this nation's foremost public opinion analyst in years gone by and again this summer mr harris

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:50.210
how much of a change has there been in the northern white man's attitudes toward the black man during

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:55.670
the summer of 1966 the fear that whites have over negroes moving into their neighborhoods is without

00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:59.650
question the deepest fear that abounds in white society today.

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:06.460
The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was fairly uncontroversial in the North.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:08.370
Same with the Voting Rights Act.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:18.770
Where it got tricky for Johnson was when the administration began to attempt to address systemic racism and systemic inequality outside of the South.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:29.890
Open housing pushed all sorts of buttons with white Americans in the North, in the Midwest, places where whites simply were not accustomed.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.900
to living in the same geographies with black neighbors,

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:43.510
that is when whites seem to say this far, but no further.

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.510
I'm sure he's only coming in here for one reason.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:48.380
That's to bust the neighborhood down,

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:50.140
discourage the white person,

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:52.340
stop the whites from moving into the area,

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:54.960
and hope that it'll be converted

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.860
to a Negro integrated development.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:26:59.020
A lot of that was driven by fear.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:26:59.970
Fear of what they...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.140
saw in neighborhoods that were predominantly african-american those neighborhoods were

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:10.120
heavily bladed those neighborhoods were dangerous there was a fair amount of crime

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:17.420
i really envy you out here in the northwest side of the city of chicago because you are very very

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:24.740
far removed from the real problems that exist you don't have slums within a block or two of you

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:29.980
if you're one of these white voters you're thinking like i don't want this in my neighborhood you're not

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:34.480
seeing what's behind it you just see something that scares you every taxpayer

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:39.790
in the United States of America has donated money to these people we can be

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:43.590
sociable with them in work we can get along with them and joke with them but

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:50.580
when we leave our job and close the door and we're on our way home we want them

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:55.750
to go in their direction and we can go in our whites were saying things like why

00:27:55.810 --> 00:27:59.920
can't you wait even whites who were supportive it's like we support the idea

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:09.260
that blacks need to to have a different life but you're pushing too fast in the 1966 election you

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:16.870
now see conservative white politicians across the country borrowing from the goldwater playbook and

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:24.660
using law and order in races for congress and democrats in general and liberals in particular

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:27.800
suffer serious, serious losses.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:37.950
are a republican elected official in 1966 you see this as a mandate to one do more of the same

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:47.760
and two increasingly to roll back the war on poverty great society initiatives that have

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:59.710
been the centerpiece of lyndon johnson's presidency at home the question is

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:05.820
whether we will continue working for better opportunities for all Americans,

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:12.580
when most Americans are already living better than many people in history.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:23.460
Abroad, the question is whether we have the staying power to fight a very costly war.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:29.980
And the objective is limited, and the danger to us is seemingly...

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:38.580
Johnson is painfully aware that the war in Vietnam is consuming 30 billion dollars a year while the

00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:42.620
expenditures on the Great Society are about a billion dollars a year and conservatives in

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:51.520
Congress are going after even that small amount. Johnson had said that we can have guns and butter

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:58.760
we don't have to choose between domestic programs and the war we can do both but in 1967 it was

00:29:58.760 --> 00:29:59.960
are becoming increasingly clear.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:00.960
that was not true.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:08.170
Then came these terrible disorders all around the country.

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:11.890
You had Newark, you had Detroit,

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.880
you had cities all across the country up in flames.

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:20.350
We're wondering, can we actually have a country?

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:23.000
Johnson's being faced with a good deal of pressure

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:27.330
to do something, but he's really incapable of governing

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:29.550
in the way that he had been accustomed to before.

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:40.340
Last week in Congress, a small but important plan for action in the cities was loaded down in the House of Representatives.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:49.610
The members of that body rejected my request for $20 million to fight the pestilence of rats.

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:56.560
Rats which prowl in dark alleys and tenons and attack thousands of city children.

00:30:58.120 --> 00:30:59.980
Moderates who pride themselves on...

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.080
their civil rights voting record because they survived the dangers of the political backlash

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:11.770
back home are now becoming backlashers themselves. Many of them seem to be saying, if Newark and

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:19.220
Detroit and Plainfield and Toledo are the thanks we get, here is our answer. Lyndon Johnson is a

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:27.310
political animal. He understands exactly what is happening. He sees the political trap that he is

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:29.530
in and he's desperately trying to find

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:37.500
way out creating a presidential commission was for johnson the best option because it allows him to

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:42.400
show presidential leadership that he's aware of the disturbances that are taking place and he's

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:47.780
doing something about it but it also allows him to kick the can down the road he can say well

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:52.820
i've done my part i've set up this distinguished commission let's wait for the recommendations

00:31:53.540 --> 00:31:59.970
I was in Mississippi doing civil rights work, and I don't know.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:12.680
I remember watching a black and white television set and Johnson speaking about this terrible crisis that was occurring in America, and he announced that he was forming this commission.

00:32:13.490 --> 00:32:24.970
We laughed and we thought, oh my God, you know, commissions are appointed to make problems go away and not to deal with them, right?

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:29.980
The commission will investigate the origins of the recent disorders in our society.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.500
cities. It will make recommendations to me, to the Congress, to the state governors and to the

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:44.480
mayors for measures to prevent or contain such disasters in the future. President Johnson called

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:54.040
us together by telegram. After he had appointed us, we met in the cabinet room. He signed the

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:59.960
executive order and gave each of us a pen. The president really was

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:05.700
He was a conciliator who would put a lot of different people from different backgrounds together and say, come, let us reason together.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.040
And they covered the political spectrum.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:14.340
They had to have supported some aspect of his great society, especially the Civil Rights Act of 64 and Voting Rights Act.

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:19.360
Also, just as important is they had to have been supporting the Vietnam War.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:22.560
He would not appoint anyone who was a skeptic of the Vietnam War.

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:29.970
It's meant to give him the political leeway to maneuver.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.630
And you anticipate him being able to pull the strings

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:37.650
and move this commission in a direction

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:39.770
that's most politically beneficial for him.

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:44.070
There were 11 members.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:49.520
The chair was Otto Koerner, who is governor of Illinois.

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:54.360
I was contacted with a telephone call the day before yesterday.

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:57.250
I want nothing but competence,

00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:59.970
and I want nothing but compassion, and I want...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.480
Nothing but patriotism.

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:04.770
John Lindsay, the mayor of New York.

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:10.840
I want you to be vice chairman, and I don't want you to even consider thinking about it.

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:12.040
I just want you to say yes, sir.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:12.720
Yes, sir.

00:34:14.220 --> 00:34:16.900
Lindsay, at that time, was still a registered Republican.

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:23.180
He was a very liberal Republican back in the old days where there were such things as liberal Republicans.

00:34:26.930 --> 00:34:29.250
There were two United States senators.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:35.300
Fred Harris, a young man who had been interested in this field in the Senate.

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:37.550
I haven't asked him yet.

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:40.880
I'm hoping that I'd get Senator Brooke from Massachusetts.

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:49.800
Ed Brooke, the first and the only then-black senator since Reconstruction.

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:52.140
A businessman.

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:56.880
I want to ask a text on the Litton Industries, California, to be on it.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:09.160
House McCulloch and Corman, a Republican and a Democrat, Herb Jenkins, the Chief of Police

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:18.120
of Atlanta, the Secretary of Commerce of the State of Kentucky, Catherine Ann Peaton, Roy Wilkins,

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:26.910
the head of the NAACP, and I.W. Abel, the head of the Steelworkers. Many very well-known people

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:28.570
and establishment people.

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:40.460
The only activist voice is that of Roy Wilkins, who, by the standards of 1967, is a fairly moderate figure.

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:48.480
Edward Brooke was the only other black person on the commission, and he was a Republican.

00:35:49.160 --> 00:35:57.680
Time magazine had given him the really questionable honorific of NASP, or Negro Anglo-Saxon Protestant.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:35:59.980
People looking at...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.960
this group, a lot of people thought that nothing really was going to come of it. And that had been

00:36:05.960 --> 00:36:13.680
true of a lot of commissions. But I think Johnson really wanted a deep and full report, as he said,

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:21.010
answered three questions. What happened? Why did it happen? What can be done to prevent it

00:36:21.160 --> 00:36:27.050
from happening again and again? Johnson said, Charlie Schultz, the budget director here,

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:29.970
will take care of whatever your needs are to do what needs.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:38.260
to be done in effect money won't be any problem for you so mr chairman mr vice chairman let your

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:46.420
search be free let us be untrammeled by what has been called the conventional wisdom as best you

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:59.620
can find the truth the whole truth and express it in your report i didn't know john lindsay

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:12.940
But in our first meeting, John spoke out about how important he thought it was that we not just deal with the question of law and order, but that we get at deeper causes.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:20.890
I, from earliest time, was very interested in the plight of poor people.

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:26.770
I grew up in a little town in Oklahoma.

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.900
I started working in the fields when I was five.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:37.710
years old. We lived in an area where Comanche Indians were all amongst us, and that, I think,

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:46.930
had some effect on me. No Negroes lived in my town. At one time, I think, were prevented from

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:53.580
doing so, but I knew how they were treated generally. John Lindsay was probably more

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:58.310
knowledgeable about these issues than anybody in America at that time.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.140
He was living with this problem as a big city mayor.

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:08.880
He saw the desperate conditions in which so many black people lived.

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:13.540
And I was convinced from the very first moment he spoke

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:16.600
that he and I were on the same wavelength exactly.

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:24.080
That first day, the very first meeting,

00:38:24.610 --> 00:38:29.900
it's clear John Lindsay and Fred Harris wanted to push the committee in.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:37.390
dealing with the root causes of racial unrest, which they believed was poverty and a sense of

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:43.850
powerlessness. Tech Thornton sees the commission's purpose solely to help law enforcement to crush

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:50.120
future uprisings. And in every debate, just about, those two are on opposite sides.

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:55.340
Governor, what is the feeling of urgency among the commission members on this first day?

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:59.960
Great urgency. As a matter of fact, if we could have continued on, I...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.460
think we would have done so. But we felt that we had to take a little breathing spell here,

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:13.240
review our charge, obtain an executive director, look at staff opportunities and places for us to

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:17.740
operate. The group decided to begin hearings in Washington next Tuesday and Wednesday with

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:22.470
government officials. The commission will ask for subpoena powers. The president wants an

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:27.370
interim report from his new commission no later than March 1st of next year. He wants a final

00:39:27.370 --> 00:39:29.830
report at the latest one year from today.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:41.470
This is not the group of people that you would generally expect to be a kind of fount of wisdom on a subject as intractable as race.

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:48.160
And that is foremost challenge that confronts them the minute they walk through the door.

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:59.250
What can they possibly say that can, one, lend insight into questions of race, and two...

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:04.380
Be instructive as to how the society can move forward.

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:15.380
Senator Thurman has some suggestions that there may be a communist conspiracy or a conspiracy involved in these riots.

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:16.680
Do you have any information on this?

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:19.300
I don't think there's any question about it.

00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:24.010
There's no doubt in my mind that these riots are organized.

00:40:25.230 --> 00:40:28.810
There's no doubt in my mind that the communists are behind these riots.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.340
Outside agitator was the phrase that people used in the South

00:40:37.340 --> 00:40:39.120
whenever anything happened that people didn't like.

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:43.540
These must be outside agitators

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:45.300
because our Negroes wouldn't behave this way.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.520
And in the North, they wouldn't go one step further.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:51.860
It was to say that the communists were behind it.

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:55.110
Surely blacks of their own accord

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:57.350
wouldn't be torturing their own neighborhoods,

00:40:57.350 --> 00:40:59.750
so there must be some hidden hand here.

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:03.420
Don't be afraid. Don't be ashamed. We want black power.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:16.270
Stokely Carmichael, a 25-year-old revolutionary, a college graduate with a degree in philosophy.

00:41:16.310 --> 00:41:26.660
It was here in Mississippi this summer that Carmichael, with his cry for black power, first became a national figure and, to many, a frightening one.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:34.240
Well, B.J. tell you that violence never accomplishes anything, my fellow Americans.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:40.320
The face of the civil rights movement has changed by the summer of 1967.

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:45.830
But you see, the real problem with violence is that we have never been violent.

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:47.850
We have been too nonviolent.

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:54.330
Dr. King remains the preeminent leader.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:41:59.980
There is a sense among white Americans...

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:02.380
that Dr. King is no longer in charge.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:05.860
You're talking about loving these honkers to death.

00:42:05.980 --> 00:42:09.580
During rebellions, brother, you've got to stop looting and start shooting.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:13.410
Of course, it alarms white people.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:15.160
It alarms the government.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:18.130
The FBI was all over these guys.

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:23.550
The communists in this country had organized very intensely

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:29.970
a drive to infiltrate into the racial discord and discontent in the country.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:39.860
Running the Federal Bureau of Investigation, J. Edgar Hoover had been a key opponent of civil rights, had collapsed civil rights with communism.

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:52.990
He understood that any form of civil rights did pose, to some degree, a legitimate challenge to the way the nation functioned.

00:42:53.090 --> 00:42:56.850
Black people had literally been raw material for wealth creation.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:42:59.970
And so Hoover's holding...

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:02.770
the line against the threat of black people

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:05.670
being at the vanguard of a communist takeover

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:06.470
of the United States.

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:15.250
Hoover is consistently playing up these possibilities,

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:19.590
sending wildly inaccurate, unvetted,

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:23.190
raw intelligence information to the White House.

00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:26.800
Because of where there's smoke, there must be some fire.

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:35.370
This issue of conspiracy was very important to the commission.

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.900
Their first major witness was J. Edgar Hoover.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:51.260
He puts all of these charts up around the room and shows Stokely Carmichael was in Maryland on this day,

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:53.260
and the next day there was a disturbance.

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:59.740
He's trying to create a connection between these advocates of black power.

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:01.950
and the racial unrests.

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:18.270
If you pressed him, J. Edgar Hoover, he said,

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:21.490
no, there was not any national conspiracy behind the riots.

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:26.140
But then he talked about outside agitators and so forth.

00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:29.980
It really made you think and made...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.400
and Johnson think that there was a conspiracy.

00:44:33.200 --> 00:44:38.890
Johnson believes Hoover and listens to Hoover.

00:44:39.910 --> 00:44:42.320
They are neighbors in Washington.

00:44:43.100 --> 00:44:46.770
Johnson is also extraordinarily thin-skinned.

00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:51.210
He takes criticism really, really personally.

00:44:52.910 --> 00:44:55.850
He is attracted to the idea that the only reason

00:44:55.850 --> 00:44:57.950
all of this violence is taking place

00:44:57.950 --> 00:44:59.930
is because people are being provoked.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:01.810
incited, and agitated.

00:45:03.300 --> 00:45:07.440
If the rebellions were about underlying racial inequalities,

00:45:07.810 --> 00:45:10.900
then that was an indictment of the war on poverty.

00:45:11.470 --> 00:45:13.310
Perhaps it hadn't gone far enough.

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:17.780
Perhaps it wasn't sufficient to address

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:19.780
historical racism and inequality.

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:37.660
Dr. King, what were the major points discussed before the commission today?

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:45.810
Well, we discussed many points concerning the civil disorders taking place.

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:54.980
My major point was that the time has come for a massive program on the part of the federal government

00:45:54.980 --> 00:45:59.900
that will make jobs or income a reality for...

00:46:00.160 --> 00:46:10.630
Every American citizen the Commission held a long series of hearings about civil disturbances and violent protests

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:23.690
20 days of hearings we heard from a lot of witnesses mission called upon people who had

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:29.630
Varying perspectives and insights into the pertinent questions of race

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:38.460
historians, sociologists, economists, law enforcement figures, activists. And they really

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:47.470
were trying to get a kind of baseline of knowledge about why American cities were on fire. One of the

00:46:47.470 --> 00:46:54.310
witnesses, the renowned black psychologist Kenneth Clark, voiced a real degree of skepticism about

00:46:54.310 --> 00:46:59.970
what they were doing. He ticks off all of the inquiries that have happened.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.320
in response to other uprisings, 1935, 1943, 1965,

00:47:08.060 --> 00:47:10.280
and says each of them produced a report.

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:13.300
None of those reports actually made any difference

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:16.600
and raises the question about whether this inquiry

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:18.040
will make any difference either.

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:21.620
So far as we can find at this point,

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:25.200
and of course we've had only a small amount of material before us

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:27.620
as to the various riots that have occurred,

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:29.920
there seems to be no common plan.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:35.540
there doesn't seem to be a plan at all something happens it may be rumor it may be an arrest but

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:42.340
suddenly it triggers the rise the president told us to state the facts and in one of the facts that

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:49.730
we said straight out was that there was a great deal of overreaction by the police and by the

00:47:49.730 --> 00:47:57.680
national guard the great majority of people who were killed were black people we looked at for

00:47:57.680 --> 00:47:59.040
For example, in sniping reports,

00:48:00.476 --> 00:48:01.696
Sniping was organized.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.940
They were mobile and able to tie up large forces.

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:07.180
there was a consistent refrain.

00:48:07.772 --> 00:48:09.772
that a sniper

00:48:10.332 --> 00:48:12.392
was shooting at

00:48:12.444 --> 00:48:13.544
or headshot.

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:18.380
law enforcement or members of the National Guard or fire departments.

00:48:20.060 --> 00:48:22.380
No one to this day will ever know.

00:48:22.940 --> 00:48:24.700
in all instances.

00:48:25.148 --> 00:48:27.148
what did happen or didn't happen.

00:48:28.092 --> 00:48:29.952
What we do know is that

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:40.140
A lot of the instances of the shooting or deaths of members of the National Guard, of the fire department, the police were by friendly fire.

00:48:40.300 --> 00:48:43.790
That being said, it didn't really matter.

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:51.400
notion of a fictionalized, larger-than-life sniper problem meant that law enforcement

00:48:51.400 --> 00:48:57.780
could use the most aggressive militarized weaponry and tactics to put down their own

00:48:57.780 --> 00:48:58.120
citizens.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:07.020
When we could trace down a sniper incident report,

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:08.650
here's the kind of thing we found.

00:49:08.810 --> 00:49:12.180
A young National Guard guy with live ammunition

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:14.880
was stationed under a streetlight.

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.600
and he was scared to death in this black neighborhood,

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:19.270
high-rise apartment nearby,

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:22.190
and he shot out the streetlight.

00:49:22.270 --> 00:49:26.390
And that gave rise to all sorts of reports

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:28.350
everywhere on the radio and everywhere else.

00:49:28.390 --> 00:49:29.410
There's snipers.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:35.030
They pulled up some kind of vehicle with a machine gun on it and sprayed that apartment

00:49:35.030 --> 00:49:44.940
as a result of what we thought was an overreaction by the National Guard, virtually all of them.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:51.400
were white and young with no training whatsoever how to deal with any civil disturbances, I

00:49:52.550 --> 00:49:59.060
suggested we make an announcement early separate from our final report calling for integration.

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:01.010
of the National Guard.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:05.600
African Americans made up less than 2%

00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:06.980
of National Guard members.

00:50:08.290 --> 00:50:10.930
Lindsey and Harris both thought that it was important

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:12.610
to make that statement.

00:50:12.750 --> 00:50:14.980
Tex Thornton doesn't want...

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:22.660
to do it. And this is the first big battle. And Tex Thornton argues vehemently against the

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:28.560
commission making any kind of a statement. We were such different people, and there were a lot of

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:29.120
different views.

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:32.860
and a lot of argument from the first,

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:38.650
I am worried that we wouldn't be able to come up with a good report.

00:50:38.890 --> 00:50:44.970
So I made a deal with the publisher to write...

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:45.500
I had to book myself.

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:57.870
We had members of the President's Commission on Civil Disorders here in our city today.

00:50:58.090 --> 00:50:59.970
We hope that they have been able to...

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.840
to walk through our city and find out for themselves exactly what exists out there.

00:51:04.940 --> 00:51:11.660
There isn't anybody that you talk to in a community that's under stress

00:51:11.660 --> 00:51:14.820
in any city of the country who is not a valuable person.

00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:23.280
to talk to. The Kerner Commission avoided this tendency to listen only to certain sections of

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:29.880
society. And they go out and they scour, trying to understand the patterns and the common...

00:51:30.028 --> 00:51:32.268
features and what is it people are saying.

00:51:32.684 --> 00:51:33.884
We can learn a good deal more.

00:51:34.412 --> 00:51:36.812
out over the country talking to people.

00:51:37.676 --> 00:51:40.176
than we can sitting in a building in Washington.

00:51:40.176 --> 00:51:41.176
And so...

00:51:41.388 --> 00:51:44.748
We're going to a good many cities around the country. Milwaukee is one of them.

00:51:45.580 --> 00:51:46.440
We just want to.

00:51:46.796 --> 00:51:50.296
kind of get the sense and feel of some of these tremendous

00:51:50.444 --> 00:51:52.344
urban problems that we have in America.

00:51:54.796 --> 00:51:58.396
In Milwaukee, I spent a morning in a black barber shop.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:06.180
young men coming in 25 to 30 and the first question i asked each of them as they came into

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:13.080
the barbershop puzzled them and i finally figured out why my question was is there greater discrimination

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:20.740
here in milwaukee than where you came from in jackson or or memphis or wherever and the reason

00:52:20.740 --> 00:52:28.510
it puzzled them was as i found out in milwaukee they didn't see any white people there was more

00:52:28.570 --> 00:52:29.490
segregation.

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:39.450
in milwaukee that's northern city than those southern cities that they'd come from and to

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:48.340
see the terrible conditions three and four families living in a one family house grocery

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:55.650
store with day-old bread and wilted vegetables, criminally inferior schools.

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:01.850
There wasn't any transportation system.

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:07.920
The jobs had left, moved out to the suburbs, or had disappeared altogether.

00:53:10.740 --> 00:53:15.060
In Cincinnati, John Lindsay and I ran onto some young men

00:53:15.060 --> 00:53:18.550
that were shooting craps on the street in broad daylight.

00:53:19.500 --> 00:53:21.580
When they found out we were from Washington,

00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:24.740
they all just, in a course almost, said,

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:27.600
Jobs, baby, we need jobs, baby.

00:53:28.320 --> 00:53:29.780
We're the only thing we can do.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:33.400
to stand around and stand on the corner while the change.

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:37.260
See, we don't have anything to do, so not no purpose.

00:53:38.400 --> 00:53:39.640
It was an eye-opener.

00:53:40.140 --> 00:53:41.400
These weren't outsiders.

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:43.060
These weren't conspirators.

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:45.160
These weren't political radicals.

00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:48.150
These were people who had legitimate

00:53:48.150 --> 00:53:50.410
and great longstanding grievances

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:52.250
about the nature of their life

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:54.590
and the limited opportunities they were provided.

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:57.150
Everybody's still all fired up about these riots

00:53:57.150 --> 00:53:58.930
and this here racial disorder and black powder.

00:53:59.050 --> 00:53:59.890
Nobody's figuring out.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.920
Half of these cats want to get out here and make it for themselves.

00:54:02.180 --> 00:54:03.760
Think they want to stay like this all their lives?

00:54:04.210 --> 00:54:06.950
The resentment is of the same kind.

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:16.430
The conviction that something must be done quickly outside of the routine channels is very widespread.

00:54:18.260 --> 00:54:24.510
It really put faces on these problems, and I know that had the same effect on other members of the commission.

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:29.970
Tex Thornton said himself, after going out...

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:36.540
to right cities and talking to people there i have moved about 90 degrees to the left

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:54.750
we also had these copious reports from our survey teams commission staff included social scientists

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:59.970
who were in washington at the headquarters and the commission

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:07.520
The nation sent out these teams of six people to 23 cities to learn exactly what had happened.

00:55:08.850 --> 00:55:12.820
They do an extraordinary field investigation.

00:55:13.460 --> 00:55:19.240
They interviewed business leaders, civic leaders, on down to black police officers.

00:55:19.940 --> 00:55:25.540
They sat in people's living rooms and barber shops. They heard their stories.

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:45.030
I was one of four analysts in charge

00:55:45.030 --> 00:55:48.010
of conducting analyses of those reports.

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:50.420
There were mountains of material

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:53.910
that we had to go through, news reports,

00:55:54.700 --> 00:55:59.160
some FBI reports, boxes and piles of documents.

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:04.380
Our job was to take that information

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:06.680
and all the statistics that we could find,

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:10.390
put them together in an analysis

00:56:10.810 --> 00:56:13.720
that answered the question, why did it happen?

00:56:15.590 --> 00:56:17.790
After having done that through about 15 cities,

00:56:18.030 --> 00:56:19.930
a common pattern began to emerge,

00:56:19.990 --> 00:56:22.510
which was, these were people protesting.

00:56:24.930 --> 00:56:27.950
In the South, you had the Jim Crow system,

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:29.980
organized legal system.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:35.420
segregation. There were lines that you couldn't cross. If you crossed them, you knew what the

00:56:35.420 --> 00:56:45.460
consequences would be. So it was possible to use civil disobedience tactics to move toward a goal.

00:56:49.700 --> 00:56:58.180
In the North, the causes of oppression and disadvantage were more opaque. You were a lot

00:56:58.180 --> 00:56:59.240
freer in many ways.

00:57:00.350 --> 00:57:07.980
But one thing that you couldn't do was move out of your constricted neighborhood, your ghetto.

00:57:08.340 --> 00:57:15.410
The six million African Americans who've moved north since World War I find that they're facing a wall of opposition.

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:18.060
Unions discriminate against them.

00:57:18.570 --> 00:57:23.410
They're not able to get the same high-paying, high-benefits union jobs that many white people did.

00:57:25.640 --> 00:57:28.560
Realtors and home sellers are discriminating against them.

00:57:28.950 --> 00:57:29.970
They are consigned to some...

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:38.040
of the worst rental properties in cities the least little thing you do it upset it unbalanced

00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:42.480
the cut and once the cut is tipped and the apples began to roll out they just keep rolling and you

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:47.900
can't pick them all back up the federal government underwrote the suburbanization of america through

00:57:48.020 --> 00:57:55.930
agencies that insured mortgages it did so through the gi bill of rights african americans who were

00:57:55.930 --> 00:57:59.970
helping to fund these programs through their taxes did not get to take part in them because of

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:03.060
discrimination among realtors and home sellers.

00:58:05.870 --> 00:58:10.420
You had a generation of young people, politically conscious.

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:15.480
Black power and black nationalism were in the air at the time.

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:23.110
They rejected the gradualism of traditional civil rights organizations.

00:58:24.050 --> 00:58:27.470
They wanted control, control of their own communities.

00:58:28.720 --> 00:58:29.920
They wanted action.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:48.960
There were long histories of efforts by black leaders to pressure city governments to address a wide variety of problems, but it never resulted in much.

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:59.800
They're going to have to find some other way to bring about change, not going through the system anymore, but turning out in the street.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:08.940
Many of the young people who participated had spent their childhoods witnessing the civil rights movement unfold.

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:18.680
Yet they were still living with roaches and rats running around in public schools that didn't have up-to-date books or resources.

00:59:18.680 --> 00:59:29.980
When nonviolent direct action protests haven't worked to change your conditions, then the next step is...

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:32.100
violent direct action.

00:59:33.870 --> 00:59:36.590
The word riot has a connotation to it.

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:40.620
The idea of riots being people destroying property,

00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:42.120
almost irrational.

00:59:43.010 --> 00:59:45.510
It doesn't suggest there's any underlying cause

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:47.390
other than bad actors, if you will.

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:51.320
But I was back in Detroit, visiting my parents.

00:59:51.680 --> 00:59:52.460
Detroit was burning.

00:59:52.910 --> 00:59:55.070
And I remember going down to the middle block

00:59:55.070 --> 00:59:58.260
and someone pointing, I think it was M16,

00:59:58.640 --> 00:59:59.340
at me and saying,

01:00:00.410 --> 01:00:01.170
Go back where you were.

01:00:03.100 --> 01:00:04.440
I was just pissed at America.

01:00:06.200 --> 01:00:08.660
That James Brown song comes to mind, you know.

01:00:10.060 --> 01:00:12.720
We'd rather die on our feet than keep living on our knees.

01:00:13.360 --> 01:00:14.680
And to me, I felt that way.

01:00:15.570 --> 01:00:19.040
I felt like, okay, yeah, there's a risk involved, but we've got to do something.

01:00:23.050 --> 01:00:29.840
Conditions were such in every one of these cities that almost any spark.

01:00:30.380 --> 01:00:31.930
could set them off.

01:00:33.100 --> 01:00:37.540
And the sparks we found were the police department.

01:00:58.900 --> 01:00:59.700
African-American.

01:01:00.190 --> 01:01:05.930
migrants who began to leave the south in first thousands and then tens of thousands and over

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:13.190
time hundreds of thousands they were leaving the south one step ahead often of racial terrors

01:01:13.410 --> 01:01:20.340
whether it came from a southern sheriff or a lynch mob and they came to these northern cities

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:28.120
in hopes that the freedom they found there would provide safety and security in addition to economic

01:01:28.120 --> 01:01:28.600
mobility.

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:34.570
Too often what they found, however,

01:01:35.030 --> 01:01:38.850
was that the police by and large were there

01:01:38.850 --> 01:01:42.150
to protect the interests of white residents.

01:01:42.470 --> 01:01:52.840
When I was researching black people

01:01:52.840 --> 01:01:55.140
moving into New York City in the 1930s,

01:01:55.260 --> 01:01:56.920
one of the things that surprised me

01:01:56.920 --> 01:01:58.900
was the number of complaints.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:03.380
from white residents about black children

01:02:03.380 --> 01:02:05.740
playing on playgrounds with their white children.

01:02:07.530 --> 01:02:09.610
And they wanted the city administrators

01:02:09.610 --> 01:02:12.090
to use the police to keep those black kids

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:13.230
off those playgrounds.

01:02:18.140 --> 01:02:21.680
This is the kind of pressure that police officers

01:02:21.680 --> 01:02:24.460
are not only getting, but they're responding to.

01:02:25.060 --> 01:02:26.160
They're doing this work.

01:02:30.280 --> 01:02:38.670
The white community treats every Black person on their block, in their store, on their playground, as a would-be suspect.

01:02:42.140 --> 01:02:50.660
Rhetoric around Black criminality was the most elastic and most durable way of justifying segregation.

01:02:52.550 --> 01:02:57.950
Police behave differently, essentially interrogating Black people at every turn.

01:03:01.920 --> 01:03:08.600
Setting up virtual checkpoints on the borders of these communities, doing

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:13.840
hostile drives through black neighborhoods as a show of force for

01:03:13.840 --> 01:03:20.980
black people to so-called stay in their place. Policing the boundaries of black

01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:27.920
life. Very often African Americans in these central cities, the contact with

01:03:27.920 --> 01:03:29.980
the police, that was

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:40.850
the only contact they had with the government and that was a contact which was really very very

01:03:41.000 --> 01:03:52.340
dangerous for them. Police brutality was a real thing but on top of that there was really no

01:03:52.340 --> 01:03:56.230
kind of procedure for grievance or for complaints.

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:05.090
That, I think, is a perfect situation waiting for an explosion.

01:04:07.010 --> 01:04:13.800
So when you see legislation like the Law Enforcement Assistance Act coming out in 1965,

01:04:14.960 --> 01:04:23.080
it effectively pours gasoline on the very problem that is at the core of these uprisings in the first place.

01:04:29.010 --> 01:04:29.970
As rebellion-esque...

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:35.440
we see a real merger happening between social welfare programs,

01:04:36.140 --> 01:04:38.600
programs specifically targeting Black youth,

01:04:39.970 --> 01:04:42.080
and crime-controlled programs

01:04:42.080 --> 01:04:44.700
that are also targeting Black youth.

01:04:45.610 --> 01:04:49.990
Police officers are running after-school recreation programs.

01:04:50.920 --> 01:04:52.860
Police started giving toys

01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:56.360
and delivering turkeys to families on Thanksgiving

01:04:56.360 --> 01:04:59.780
and to essentially cast a layer of surveillance.

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:13.010
It was about making sure that you could identify people in the black community who posed a potential threat to the neighboring white communities, taking down the names of potential delinquents.

01:05:14.210 --> 01:05:29.370
The police also supported tactical patrol units who walked the streets in order to prevent crime before it occurred, essentially subjecting people of color to a set of laws.

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:34.320
that people in middle-class, suburban, and white communities

01:05:34.320 --> 01:05:35.920
would never be subjected to.

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:38.400
They would actually stop people

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:41.900
for bumping the tires against the curb when they're parking.

01:05:42.160 --> 01:05:44.570
Spitting, part of it is sending a message.

01:05:45.360 --> 01:05:47.810
It's sending a message that we're in control.

01:05:48.230 --> 01:05:49.810
You're supposed to be afraid of us,

01:05:50.660 --> 01:05:53.880
and we can stop you for any reason.

01:05:59.650 --> 01:05:59.970
They're out.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:01.640
out on the street all the time.

01:06:02.410 --> 01:06:04.230
There are these young people on the street.

01:06:05.770 --> 01:06:09.670
The police are trying to keep them moving,

01:06:09.870 --> 01:06:13.690
keep them off balance by all of these harassing tactics.

01:06:14.140 --> 01:06:17.240
Stop and frisk, make arbitrary arrests,

01:06:17.420 --> 01:06:20.020
and then they'd be released without charges.

01:06:21.290 --> 01:06:22.830
There's this constant conflict,

01:06:23.360 --> 01:06:25.460
constant daily rubbing conflict

01:06:26.260 --> 01:06:29.970
between police and young people in the street.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:36.890
streets. I have more fear for the police than I have for the criminal because the policeman can do

01:06:36.890 --> 01:06:46.950
it and get away with it. I can fight the criminal back. There often comes a conspicuous incident of

01:06:46.950 --> 01:06:59.870
police brutality, an outrageous act by police. In Detroit, it was a raid on an after-hours social club.

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:04.220
People were partying to celebrate the return

01:07:04.220 --> 01:07:05.920
of one veteran from Vietnam

01:07:06.770 --> 01:07:10.530
and console another guy who was gonna head to Vietnam.

01:07:14.810 --> 01:07:19.030
In Newark, it was the beating of a cab driver.

01:07:20.650 --> 01:07:23.250
When residents observe what's happening,

01:07:23.680 --> 01:07:25.260
they decide enough is enough.

01:07:27.490 --> 01:07:29.980
I'm in time again, somewhere in there.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:36.730
story is this moment of exchange, this interaction between a police officer and a black person.

01:07:36.850 --> 01:07:45.300
It was an endless cycle, the wave of urban violence followed by calls for law and order. Law and order

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:50.120
would then express itself in a heavy-handed way and this was often the spark behind the next wave

01:07:50.120 --> 01:07:59.540
of violence. When Detroit and Newark erupt in 67, two years into the war on crime, that should have

01:07:59.540 --> 01:07:59.860
been

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:06.770
in as a moment for policymakers to rethink these strategies, but policymakers responded

01:08:06.770 --> 01:08:15.020
by militarizing police so that when and if rebellion comes, police departments will be

01:08:15.020 --> 01:08:17.940
ready to suppress the disorder on their own.

01:08:19.310 --> 01:08:27.240
We began to see local police getting armored tanks, getting M1 and M14 carbine rifles, using

01:08:27.240 --> 01:08:29.980
helicopters, all techniques and devices that had

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:31.560
been used in Vietnam.

01:08:32.650 --> 01:08:40.420
We very much were opposed to the military giving military hardware and vehicles to the police.

01:08:41.900 --> 01:08:45.000
We thought that the police ought to be civilians.

01:08:46.440 --> 01:08:51.540
They should look like and be a part of the communities where they served.

01:08:53.030 --> 01:08:58.600
Lyndon Johnson himself once had said, if I were one of these people and mistreated the

01:08:58.600 --> 01:08:59.980
way some of them are.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:07.660
by the police i might pick up a gun myself later on i'm sure he was sorry he had said that but

01:09:11.490 --> 01:09:27.960
that's the way we felt estimated

01:09:30.030 --> 01:09:51.500
We spend $322,000 for each enemy we kill in Vietnam, while we spend in the so-called war on poverty in America only about $53 for each person classified as poor.

01:09:55.460 --> 01:09:59.980
Absent the conflict in Southeast Asia, Lyndon Johnson would go down as...

01:10:00.160 --> 01:10:02.500
an extraordinarily gifted leader,

01:10:03.980 --> 01:10:06.420
but Vietnam is the albatross

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:10.040
that will always hang over Lyndon Johnson's legacy.

01:10:10.440 --> 01:10:12.920
I report to you that our country is challenged

01:10:12.920 --> 01:10:14.200
at home and abroad,

01:10:16.100 --> 01:10:19.060
that it is our will that is being tried

01:10:19.060 --> 01:10:21.230
and not our strength.

01:10:33.290 --> 01:10:37.930
As he began to lose the hearts and minds of young people, of people on college campuses,

01:10:37.930 --> 01:10:42.350
as the war became a divisive factor in American life,

01:10:42.350 --> 01:10:47.270
he was loath to admit how deeply involved the United States was in Vietnam.

01:10:47.740 --> 01:10:55.600
I wish with all of my heart that the expenditures that are necessary to build and to protect our power

01:10:55.600 --> 01:10:58.570
could all be devoted to the programs of peace.

01:10:59.100 --> 01:10:59.980
He remembered other...

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.870
Democratic presidents who saw their domestic policy ambitions cut short by wars that ultimately

01:11:05.870 --> 01:11:11.210
made it impossible for them to continue to pursue those goals. He didn't want that to happen to the

01:11:11.210 --> 01:11:20.490
Great Society. Johnson is also hoping desperately that the Kerner Commission will acknowledge that

01:11:20.490 --> 01:11:26.090
his Great Society is actually making a difference and improving the lives of Black Americans across

01:11:26.090 --> 01:11:29.890
the country. But the Kerner Commission quickly becomes a

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:36.800
runaway commission certainly uh in speaking to people who are sociologists who've been at this

01:11:36.800 --> 01:11:42.510
thing much longer than i have and who've made it their specialty i have all pointed out the fact

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:49.700
that there is a a feeling of deep inferiority among these people they've been rejected as to

01:11:49.700 --> 01:11:59.250
housing as to job and after job as to improving the status of the job and i do have a feeling

01:11:59.250 --> 01:11:59.470
myself.

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:06.340
and again, I'm speaking personally, that the white community of the United States is not aware

01:12:06.340 --> 01:12:14.020
of the existence of this problem. I'm afraid many are. And many who are aware of it are not aware

01:12:14.020 --> 01:12:21.780
as to how deep and sensitive this is. Johnson was getting all the time inside reports,

01:12:23.080 --> 01:12:29.320
some from some of the members of the commission itself, who thought that John Lindsay and I

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:38.090
we're going too far, and that we were going to recommend huge new federal programs and not put

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:44.140
any money figures on them. And that angered him a great deal. I was later to find out.

01:12:45.140 --> 01:12:51.020
That's when Lyndon Johnson becomes acutely aware of what a political bombshell this might

01:12:51.020 --> 01:12:56.300
represent. And as soon as the Kerner Commission becomes perceived as a potential threat to him

01:12:56.300 --> 01:12:59.740
politically, I think he wants to take the legs out from under it.

01:13:01.880 --> 01:13:05.740
My assumption was this was a national crisis, this was the federal government,

01:13:06.260 --> 01:13:07.760
money was not going to be the problem.

01:13:08.880 --> 01:13:12.960
My role on the commission was to be the assistant to Victor Palmieri,

01:13:13.120 --> 01:13:16.640
the deputy executive director who was responsible for the day-to-day operations.

01:13:18.050 --> 01:13:22.840
In early December, we were invited to the White House to meet with Charlie Schultz,

01:13:22.900 --> 01:13:25.060
who was then the director of the Bureau of the Budget,

01:13:25.570 --> 01:13:29.970
who had the task of informing us that we were going to have to close the commission.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:36.450
down as quickly as we could. Victor and I a couple weeks before Christmas had to

01:13:36.450 --> 01:13:41.940
have a meeting with by then 200 members of our staff to advise them that most of

01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:47.500
them were not going to be on the payroll after January 1st. David Ginsberg for

01:13:47.500 --> 01:13:53.930
whom I worked was the director. He tried very much to protect Johnson even

01:13:53.930 --> 01:13:58.930
though he knew the truth by saying that the Commission's work was so important.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:04.730
that we were going to get out a report months, months earlier

01:14:04.730 --> 01:14:09.160
in order to have our views before the American people

01:14:09.160 --> 01:14:11.860
before the hot weather of the next summer.

01:14:13.960 --> 01:14:15.040
Well, that was baloney.

01:14:15.260 --> 01:14:17.720
Governor, some of the ex-staff members of the commission

01:14:17.720 --> 01:14:19.900
are all over the hill complaining to Congress

01:14:19.900 --> 01:14:23.500
that the president or some of the White House people

01:14:23.500 --> 01:14:25.600
are torpedoing the work of the commission.

01:14:25.780 --> 01:14:29.120
Well, let me say there is absolutely not an iota of truth in that.

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:34.170
This March 1 deadline is a self-imposed deadline by the commission.

01:14:34.890 --> 01:14:39.250
And the reason the commission came to that conclusion was because of the urgency of the problem.

01:14:40.020 --> 01:14:45.880
But it's a very difficult timetable to live with because of the mountains of material that we're working with.

01:14:46.990 --> 01:14:49.210
What we had to do was start drafting.

01:14:55.360 --> 01:14:59.970
We had eight or ten different people, some people working on the introduction, others working on...

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:01.980
on various programmatic sections.

01:15:02.820 --> 01:15:05.820
And all of that had to be put into readable form

01:15:06.300 --> 01:15:07.580
within a matter of days.

01:15:08.560 --> 01:15:10.980
David Ginsberg and Vic Palmieri

01:15:10.980 --> 01:15:14.320
were primarily responsible for writing these drafts.

01:15:14.900 --> 01:15:18.980
And we discussed and then actually voted on each draft.

01:15:21.070 --> 01:15:23.830
All of us were people of very strong opinions.

01:15:24.230 --> 01:15:27.770
And so our meetings were quite contentious.

01:15:28.470 --> 01:15:29.730
The commissioners argued

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:32.600
over the significance of social conditions

01:15:33.080 --> 01:15:34.760
in creating the uprisings,

01:15:35.550 --> 01:15:37.970
how forcefully to condemn the police,

01:15:38.360 --> 01:15:40.960
how strongly to support open housing.

01:15:41.360 --> 01:15:43.400
And as each of those is resolved,

01:15:43.540 --> 01:15:47.340
it sends the commission kind of further down the path

01:15:47.560 --> 01:15:49.200
toward its ultimate conclusions.

01:15:50.390 --> 01:15:52.750
Probably the most significant chapter

01:15:52.750 --> 01:15:59.860
was the fourth chapter that was drafted by David Ginsberg.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:05.050
himself, after sensing a consensus among the commissioners

01:16:05.050 --> 01:16:08.580
that the central theme of the report

01:16:08.580 --> 01:16:13.000
should be the impacts of white racism on black people.

01:16:13.730 --> 01:16:17.230
And that was the subject of a whole day's commissioners

01:16:17.230 --> 01:16:17.470
meeting.

01:16:18.400 --> 01:16:22.450
I remember the commission spending well over an hour

01:16:22.450 --> 01:16:24.710
haggling over two sentences.

01:16:26.280 --> 01:16:28.980
I thought, and so did John Lindsay,

01:16:28.980 --> 01:16:29.900
that it was

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:39.760
highly important to use the word racism, to say white racism. We thought we really needed

01:16:39.760 --> 01:16:48.290
to shock people by the use of that term and to make people have to think about that and talk

01:16:48.290 --> 01:16:55.860
about it and size up history in terms of racism as it had existed from the first.

01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:09.200
We also wanted to say to young black people, particularly, you're not crazy.

01:17:10.580 --> 01:17:14.400
There is systematic racism that you're a victim of.

01:17:16.720 --> 01:17:21.790
The word racism didn't sit well with some members of the commission.

01:17:22.610 --> 01:17:29.970
Some thought it was too harsh, that we ought to use softer words like intolerance or discrimination.

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:36.880
discrimination or whatever. And we had a vote. It was a six to five vote within the commission

01:17:36.880 --> 01:17:39.090
in favor of saying racism.

01:17:41.440 --> 01:17:44.160
Governor, there are measures that won't cost any money.

01:17:46.650 --> 01:17:50.990
Yes, there are recommendations in there that would not cost any money, and they're the

01:17:50.990 --> 01:17:57.990
measures that would do away with racism. We believe by actually setting forth to the American

01:17:57.990 --> 01:17:59.950
people that there is this problem.

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:06.220
Let's not sweep it under the rug, that there is segregation, that if we can change people's

01:18:06.220 --> 01:18:08.380
attitudes, this will not cost pennies.

01:18:10.490 --> 01:18:14.360
On a lot of tough questions, we voted six to five.

01:18:15.070 --> 01:18:19.880
But when we got down to the final report, we eventually adopted it unanimously.

01:18:22.260 --> 01:18:29.970
David Ginsburg said once that this is the only unanimous report that was adopted.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:31.520
by a vote of 6 to 5.

01:18:32.420 --> 01:18:37.520
There was a lot of compromise involved.

01:18:38.400 --> 01:18:40.830
We thought, if we can't come together,

01:18:41.420 --> 01:18:43.420
there's no hope the country can.

01:18:45.250 --> 01:18:48.560
Only if those who were most conservative

01:18:48.560 --> 01:18:53.490
on the commission endorsed the strong findings

01:18:53.490 --> 01:18:56.310
that we had made would it have an effect

01:18:56.780 --> 01:18:58.910
on white America in general.

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:04.300
And the report was written for white America.

01:19:06.140 --> 01:19:11.100
Black people didn't need to be told that racism was a central problem in their lives.

01:19:11.260 --> 01:19:20.030
I trust that the report will be thoroughly readable and expect that it will be completely honest.

01:19:21.980 --> 01:19:26.740
That being the case, it will describe undoubtedly a very serious condition in the country.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:34.680
Commission's view, undoubtedly, will be that no one can turn their back on that. It's too serious.

01:19:37.120 --> 01:19:44.480
We had well in advance decided that we wanted to try to get this out popularly to as many people

01:19:44.480 --> 01:19:52.580
as possible. And David Ginsburg, our executive director, was very pression about this and so

01:19:52.580 --> 01:19:59.700
made arrangements with Bantam Press that as we finished one chapter, we would turn that over.

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:03.820
so that they could get it out exactly when the report was finished.

01:20:04.780 --> 01:20:15.450
The other thing we did was to put the report out with an embargo for the AMs on the morning of March 3rd.

01:20:16.200 --> 01:20:21.480
We wanted time to background the reporters, journalists, and opinion leaders,

01:20:22.230 --> 01:20:29.960
but Alan Spivek, our press secretary, got word from a Washington Post reporter that the Washington Post...

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:38.920
The Post had a copy of the summary of the report, and they intended to turn it loose for February 29.

01:20:39.870 --> 01:20:44.340
So David Ginsberg talked to Ben Bradley, the Post, said to him,

01:20:44.540 --> 01:20:52.910
if you do this, we are going to turn loose the summary ourselves so that you won't have an exclusive on it.

01:20:54.830 --> 01:20:55.970
There was just chaos.

01:20:57.170 --> 01:20:59.970
Reporters here, they're looking at all this, and they don't have...

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:06.790
time to digest it. I got a call from an AP reporter who said, I have a 30-minute deadline.

01:21:06.990 --> 01:21:14.510
Can you sort of capsulize the report for me? The headlines were very shocking headlines,

01:21:15.200 --> 01:21:19.900
particularly if you'd not thought about this before or known anything about it, as most people

01:21:19.980 --> 01:21:23.600
didn't know much about it. The headlines were something like,

01:21:23.960 --> 01:21:28.980
White racism, cause of black riots, commission says.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:35.120
This is Harry Reisner. For the last few days, this country has lived under indictment,

01:21:35.420 --> 01:21:41.320
a charge of white racism, national in scale, terrible in its effects. The evidence to support

01:21:41.320 --> 01:21:47.360
that charge has now been presented. More than 1,400 pages of testimony, findings, conclusions,

01:21:47.900 --> 01:21:54.070
the full text of a report released just last night. This is our basic conclusion. Our nation

01:21:54.070 --> 01:21:59.880
is moving toward two societies, one black, one white, separate.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:00.760
and unequal.

01:22:02.160 --> 01:22:05.880
The commission found that the country is at a crossroads

01:22:05.880 --> 01:22:10.680
and that a choice can be made and must be made.

01:22:11.540 --> 01:22:14.220
You had to really be hibernating somewhere,

01:22:14.240 --> 01:22:17.880
not to see the media coverage in the press and on television.

01:22:18.220 --> 01:22:20.980
But if you wanted to know more in detail,

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:24.840
the fact that you could go to a store and buy the paperback

01:22:25.020 --> 01:22:27.940
allowed a million and a half people within a month

01:22:27.940 --> 01:22:29.960
to have the actual report in there.

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:39.560
hand. We said that white racism has created these black ghettos, as we said in those days,

01:22:40.000 --> 01:22:49.430
and it's white racism that sustains it. This is shocking to the sensibilities in 1968. There had

01:22:49.430 --> 01:22:58.710
been large-scale studies that indicted American racism prior to this. W.E.B. Du Bois had done one,

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:07.040
know, nearly 70 years earlier. What we had not seen was this kind of argument being echoed

01:23:07.540 --> 01:23:14.190
in the highest echelons of American power. It was very important to the commission that

01:23:14.190 --> 01:23:21.900
people in the United States understand that more was going on than that just some of us

01:23:21.900 --> 01:23:29.900
individuals didn't like black people. It was that kind of change that was necessary.

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:33.540
involves a changing of whole institutions.

01:23:36.020 --> 01:23:39.220
You're looking at a report that's basically identifying

01:23:39.220 --> 01:23:41.760
a whole range of public and private actors

01:23:41.760 --> 01:23:44.840
who have been responsible for perpetuating racism,

01:23:45.020 --> 01:23:48.500
from the banks to the mortgage providers and the realtors

01:23:48.500 --> 01:23:50.860
to public and private sector unions

01:23:50.860 --> 01:23:52.900
to municipal police forces

01:23:52.900 --> 01:23:54.980
and municipal governments more broadly.

01:23:56.130 --> 01:23:58.210
This is the first time you had the government saying,

01:23:58.390 --> 01:23:59.890
these problems are systemic.

01:24:00.090 --> 01:24:02.530
Certain people have benefited from the system.

01:24:02.850 --> 01:24:04.670
Certain people have perpetuated the system.

01:24:04.790 --> 01:24:06.730
And other people have been disadvantaged.

01:24:07.070 --> 01:24:10.010
And to fix it, we are probably going to have to rebalance the books.

01:24:11.690 --> 01:24:15.230
Whites were saying things like, what's wrong with the Negro in the country?

01:24:15.510 --> 01:24:16.950
What's wrong with black people in the country?

01:24:17.150 --> 01:24:18.270
How do we fix them?

01:24:19.810 --> 01:24:21.290
What the Kernan Commission did was say,

01:24:22.190 --> 01:24:26.600
the problem is not so much with them, the problem is with us.

01:24:28.220 --> 01:24:29.980
Here in the Populist Central Wall,

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:34.300
a Negro district, the Special Commission's report was greeted as an acknowledgement

01:24:34.300 --> 01:24:38.260
of what Negroes themselves have been saying in Newark for several years.

01:24:39.470 --> 01:24:45.440
The Kerner Commission united the political and economic abandonment of a group of people

01:24:45.440 --> 01:24:47.990
with the cause of American democracy.

01:24:47.990 --> 01:24:49.530
They say this forthrightly.

01:24:49.710 --> 01:24:54.180
They say, our collective future is in peril.

01:24:55.160 --> 01:24:58.720
Pursue our present course will involve the continuing polarization.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:05.600
of the american community and ultimately the destruction of basic democratic values

01:25:05.600 --> 01:25:14.740
the alternative will require a commitment to national action passionate massive and sustained

01:25:14.740 --> 01:25:21.660
backed by the resources of the most powerful and richest nation on the earth from every american

01:25:21.660 --> 01:25:28.720
that will require new attitudes new understanding and above all new will the kerner commission comes

01:25:28.720 --> 01:25:29.970
down and says we don't

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:36.310
have to be two Americas. We can't be two Americas. We can fix this. But first of all,

01:25:38.330 --> 01:25:43.340
we have to acknowledge it. And what we've tried to do in this report is to let people see this

01:25:43.340 --> 01:25:47.330
thing through our eyes and to feel it in the pit of their stomachs like we do. And then if they

01:25:47.330 --> 01:25:51.910
see what a crisis this really is, what a terribly serious crisis this is for our country, I think

01:25:51.910 --> 01:25:56.580
there'll be a great deal more willingness to try to move to do something about it. We call for

01:25:56.580 --> 01:25:58.980
massive new federal programs.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:07.030
in particular but also an education and in housing and in transportation and we

01:26:07.030 --> 01:26:11.130
call for changes in the media and in the police.

01:26:11.430 --> 01:26:16.210
An integrated police force is a must, says the Commission, a visible symbol for the

01:26:16.210 --> 01:26:19.730
ghetto that police are not just the agents of repression put there by the

01:26:19.730 --> 01:26:26.500
white majority. The Commission suggested all sorts of measures to promote police

01:26:26.500 --> 01:26:29.820
community relations, to increase trust between residents.

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:38.260
police and to kind of scale back some of the more aggressive enforcement approaches that had led to

01:26:38.260 --> 01:26:44.370
the rebellions in the first place. These are the major proposals on welfare a new approach a form

01:26:44.370 --> 01:26:50.060
of guaranteed annual income based on the idea that even in good times some people can't get jobs or

01:26:50.060 --> 01:26:56.260
can't make enough to support their families on housing a national open housing law six million

01:26:56.260 --> 01:26:59.120
new low- and middle-income units on jobs.

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:02.280
in new jobs in government and private business.

01:27:02.630 --> 01:27:05.730
The cost has been estimated at $2 billion a month,

01:27:06.410 --> 01:27:08.310
something like the cost of the war in Vietnam.

01:27:09.410 --> 01:27:11.070
The Kerner Commission recognized

01:27:11.070 --> 01:27:15.080
that the war on poverty had not gone far enough,

01:27:15.760 --> 01:27:20.700
that what was needed was a massive structural transformation

01:27:20.700 --> 01:27:25.120
to address the underlying causes of the rebellions.

01:27:26.260 --> 01:27:29.760
The report, in many ways, is a really bold...

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:35.230
an exciting vision for what U.S. domestic policy could be.

01:27:38.420 --> 01:27:43.020
This is Bushwick, a neighborhood in Brooklyn that's half black and half white.

01:27:43.380 --> 01:27:50.160
At PS 151, where the pupils are mostly Negro or Puerto Rican, the school's book fair was

01:27:50.160 --> 01:27:53.560
enlarged to include a table full of the paper-bound reports.

01:27:54.290 --> 01:27:56.470
Sixth graders were not the best customers.

01:27:56.690 --> 01:27:59.970
But throughout the neighborhood, in various ways, more than...

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:06.800
2,000 copies of the riot report were sold in less than a week it was a new york times bestseller and

01:28:06.800 --> 01:28:13.300
it's a tone it's not light reading it's not a comic book so there was interest it really becomes the

01:28:13.300 --> 01:28:20.600
centerpiece of conversation you see civic groups churches libraries even police departments buying

01:28:20.660 --> 01:28:29.700
the book in bulk at one point marlon brando who was then an a-list figure in hollywood

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:35.780
made the rounds on talk shows carrying a copy of the Kerner Commission report with him.

01:28:36.090 --> 01:28:47.230
I mean, this report clearly indicates that the black people have not been able to pick themselves up by their bootstraps

01:28:47.230 --> 01:28:49.590
because of white feelings against them.

01:28:50.160 --> 01:28:53.280
And we, all of us, have to find out what is that.

01:28:53.910 --> 01:28:55.570
What's going to be the answer to this report?

01:28:56.110 --> 01:28:58.210
What answer in the form of action in Washington?

01:28:59.560 --> 01:28:59.980
Here's Roger.

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:03.440
Roger Mudd, who covers Congress. Roger, what's the impact down there?

01:29:04.460 --> 01:29:13.750
Harry, the report has not been accepted on Capitol Hill as gospel, as it has been in most of the editorial reaction in the public prints.

01:29:14.430 --> 01:29:22.240
The mentality of Capitol Hill is not geared to instant reaction to broad programs, broad studies.

01:29:22.760 --> 01:29:28.390
It's geared to specific legislative proposals. As yet, nothing has come from the White House but silence.

01:29:28.720 --> 01:29:29.980
When the report was issued...

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:34.060
Johnson already had a good sense of what was in it.

01:29:35.420 --> 01:29:39.560
His reaction was the most negative of any president

01:29:39.560 --> 01:29:42.900
who ever received a report from a commission he'd created.

01:29:43.580 --> 01:29:45.840
He refused to accept a copy.

01:29:46.560 --> 01:29:49.120
He refused to thank the commissioners.

01:29:49.610 --> 01:29:53.450
He refused to hold what had already become a custom,

01:29:53.780 --> 01:29:56.760
receiving it from them and thanking them for it,

01:29:56.880 --> 01:29:59.980
maybe having everybody appear on the White House lawn.

01:30:17.870 --> 01:30:25.880
We didn't really think about whether Lyndon Johnson would like what we said or found or recommended.

01:30:26.710 --> 01:30:29.780
He said, find the truth and express it in your report.

01:30:31.210 --> 01:30:40.260
That's what we did, and we were really disappointed, surprised that he rejected it.

01:30:41.700 --> 01:30:46.650
Every time you appoint one of these committees, you get more than you can do anything about.

01:30:46.830 --> 01:30:51.110
They recommended that I spend $80 million and I've got no place to get the $80.

01:30:51.150 --> 01:30:55.570
I can't borrow it, I can't tax it, I can't get a tax bill of any kind,

01:30:56.020 --> 01:30:58.960
and they didn't discuss that. That's a detail they didn't get into.

01:31:01.820 --> 01:31:03.880
Johnson was just in a mess.

01:31:05.760 --> 01:31:10.100
The Ted Offensive had happened in January of that year.

01:31:10.520 --> 01:31:14.400
We have been too often disappointed by the optimism of the American leaders,

01:31:14.400 --> 01:31:16.040
both in Vietnam and Washington,

01:31:16.440 --> 01:31:21.340
to have faith any longer in the silver linings they find in the darkest clouds.

01:31:22.030 --> 01:31:24.050
For it seems now more certain than ever

01:31:24.050 --> 01:31:28.490
that the bloody experience of Vietnam is to end in a stalemate.

01:31:36.520 --> 01:31:42.290
There were increasing demonstrations all around the country and right outside the White House

01:31:42.760 --> 01:31:48.160
with people saying, LBJ, how many kids did you kill today?

01:31:51.330 --> 01:31:54.390
His own popularity had sunk to 36%.

01:31:54.390 --> 01:31:59.700
I've not wanted to reflect on Turner and criticize the commission.

01:32:00.480 --> 01:32:07.440
At the same time, I couldn't embrace it because I've got to have some fiscal solvents and I can't have it unless I have a tax bill.

01:32:08.590 --> 01:32:11.370
We're not talking about something that is going to kill the country.

01:32:12.440 --> 01:32:22.140
Over a five-year period, we put $18 billion into highways alone, another $18 to $20 billion into the space program.

01:32:23.220 --> 01:32:29.500
Now, is this country going to say that it's impossible to correct the cancer that exists in the center of these?

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:30.840
of these cities.

01:32:31.640 --> 01:32:33.480
So it's bad enough that it's a presidential commission

01:32:33.480 --> 01:32:35.180
and it's Johnson's presidential commission,

01:32:35.200 --> 01:32:38.700
but it's also populated with leading white liberal

01:32:38.700 --> 01:32:41.520
politicians who are effectively telling white America,

01:32:41.680 --> 01:32:43.640
you're responsible for the racism,

01:32:43.880 --> 01:32:45.180
you're responsible for the violence,

01:32:45.300 --> 01:32:46.920
you're responsible for what you see on TV.

01:32:47.830 --> 01:32:50.430
And this is in the lead up to the 1968 elections.

01:32:51.920 --> 01:32:53.720
Johnson was already conducting a fair amount

01:32:53.720 --> 01:32:54.560
of public polling.

01:32:55.260 --> 01:32:56.980
Richard Scammon, who was his pollster,

01:32:57.590 --> 01:32:59.980
looked at the electorate and came back

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:06.740
to him and said, look, the median voter in 1968 is un-young, un-black, and un-poor. And it

01:33:06.740 --> 01:33:13.280
fundamentally created a political problem for Johnson. The great irony of the Kerner Commission

01:33:13.280 --> 01:33:20.940
is that Johnson sets it up as a way of building support for his presidency. But in the end,

01:33:21.060 --> 01:33:26.010
it puts him in an impossible position. He can't accept the recommendations without

01:33:26.010 --> 01:33:29.980
out alienating the conservative members of Congress, he

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:38.410
also can't criticize it without alienating liberals he can't please anyone there was also a sense that

01:33:38.590 --> 01:33:43.080
he had run out of steam and run out of capital he liked being president to do things and it was

01:33:43.080 --> 01:33:49.260
by no means clear that were he able to eke out another term he'd be able to expand on the great

01:33:49.260 --> 01:33:54.840
society or build bigger domestic policy programs in all likelihood he would have had to just

01:33:54.840 --> 01:33:59.980
continue what he was doing, which was managing unruly nation at home and a war that...

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:01.720
that seemed impossible to get out of abroad.

01:34:04.010 --> 01:34:06.370
I think Johnson sort of felt increasingly,

01:34:06.510 --> 01:34:07.630
where are my allies?

01:34:09.520 --> 01:34:12.060
People are failing to appreciate that, in fact,

01:34:12.200 --> 01:34:15.180
I did do more for this country than many other presidents.

01:34:17.390 --> 01:34:20.550
With America's sons in the field far away,

01:34:23.430 --> 01:34:27.180
with America's future under challenge right here at home,

01:34:27.940 --> 01:34:28.960
I do not believe.

01:34:30.600 --> 01:34:37.230
that I should devote an hour or a day of my time to any personal partisan causes

01:34:37.230 --> 01:34:45.870
or to any duties other than the awesome duties of this office.

01:34:46.960 --> 01:34:59.380
Accordingly, I shall not seek and I will not accept the nomination of my party for another term as your president.

01:35:09.500 --> 01:35:17.960
Ladies and gentlemen, I'm only going to talk to you just for a minute or so this evening

01:35:17.960 --> 01:35:23.750
because I have some very sad news for all of you

01:35:23.750 --> 01:35:29.930
and I think sad news for all of our fellow citizens and people.

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:37.340
who love peace all over the world and that is that martin luther king was shot and was killed

01:35:37.340 --> 01:35:59.050
tonight so many americans felt this sense of

01:36:01.340 --> 01:36:05.120
our last best hope for the prospects of nonviolence

01:36:05.120 --> 01:36:06.680
has been taken away from us.

01:36:28.020 --> 01:36:29.900
In the aftermath of King's assassination,

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:33.960
when you see a replay of the kind of violence

01:36:33.960 --> 01:36:36.460
that had taken place in the summer of 1967,

01:36:37.260 --> 01:36:41.430
it feels as though the Kerner Commission's prophecies

01:36:41.430 --> 01:36:44.190
are simply being fulfilled.

01:36:44.570 --> 01:36:52.400
At least 100 cities erupted in the days and weeks after.

01:36:54.120 --> 01:36:55.760
Martin Luther King, the king of love,

01:36:55.980 --> 01:36:57.660
the king of nonviolence and peace,

01:36:58.470 --> 01:36:59.910
for him to get assassinated.

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:08.120
really made the entire future of black Americans seem as uncertain as ever.

01:37:11.750 --> 01:37:14.610
Country is truly in flames.

01:37:16.960 --> 01:37:22.130
The U.S. military has to send thousands and thousands of troops to the Capitol

01:37:22.130 --> 01:37:24.570
to protect Congress and the White House.

01:37:25.130 --> 01:37:29.640
You have angry and fearful members of Congress pleading with Johnson.

01:37:30.190 --> 01:37:34.730
to issue shoot-to-kill orders to the U.S. soldiers who were in Washington, D.C.

01:37:41.300 --> 01:37:46.240
We were all, all of us, extremely depressed about the assassination that had just occurred.

01:37:46.240 --> 01:37:52.830
But I also felt, well, this is, of course, exactly what you expect, right?

01:37:53.520 --> 01:37:59.550
And later that night, I went downtown, and as we turned off of...

01:38:00.090 --> 01:38:07.340
14th Street, we were looking straight ahead at a tank coming toward us.

01:38:08.370 --> 01:38:18.560
And I remember thinking, this just can't be the America that we live in.

01:38:21.210 --> 01:38:26.010
Soon after the National Advisory Commission on Civil Disorders published its report last month,

01:38:26.760 --> 01:38:29.980
members of the commission received a telegram which concluded with the...

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:36.520
following words my only hope now is that white america and our national government will heed

01:38:36.600 --> 01:38:43.300
your warnings and implement your recommendations by ignoring them we will sink inevitably into

01:38:43.480 --> 01:38:49.410
a nightmarish racial doomsday god grant that your excellent report will educate the nation

01:38:49.410 --> 01:38:55.670
and lead to action before it is too late the telegram was signed by martin luther king jr

01:38:56.560 --> 01:38:59.930
now just six weeks after that telegram was sent

01:39:01.150 --> 01:39:04.510
It's too late to answer the martyred Dr. King.

01:39:05.420 --> 01:39:12.450
It could be that out of this tragedy, as sorrowful as it has left most of us,

01:39:13.080 --> 01:39:21.880
would come a jolt, a kind of a realization that we cannot continue, again to quote the report, on the present course.

01:39:26.460 --> 01:39:29.760
Liberals in Congress have been trying to pass the Fair Housing Act.

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:35.440
years. But in the wake of the King assassination, in light of the report's recommendations,

01:39:36.000 --> 01:39:44.920
there's new momentum. So if there's one significant piece of legislation that is the direct result of

01:39:44.920 --> 01:39:51.470
the Kerner Commission, it's the Fair Housing Act of 1968. But those who know the Fair Housing Act

01:39:51.470 --> 01:39:59.450
of 1968 well know that it is at best a toothless attempt to deal with the worst aspects of housing

01:39:59.450 --> 01:39:59.710
discrimination.

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:11.800
It doesn't begin to address the fundamental things that are keeping blacks from being able to break out of what someone called an encircling noose, these white suburbs around black inner city areas.

01:40:12.300 --> 01:40:13.840
It's too little and too late.

01:40:15.370 --> 01:40:29.970
When I ask myself what the commissioners thought would be the impact of the report and whether it would produce the kind of change that they were calling for, I suspect very few of them were naive enough to believe.

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:40.730
believe that, well, goodbye racism. We're going to have a new world here. I think they just hoped it

01:40:40.730 --> 01:40:48.460
would be a small nudge in the right direction. The report did have its impacts here and there.

01:40:48.940 --> 01:40:53.940
For instance, the commission had critiqued the media saying they tended to look at these issues

01:40:53.940 --> 01:40:59.960
through the eyes of white men. And you did see news organizations diversifying.

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:08.970
their ranks. They didn't pledge to cover urban issues with more nuance and more balance. But by

01:41:08.970 --> 01:41:18.170
and large, the political and moral will that the report had hoped to summon, it doesn't really

01:41:18.170 --> 01:41:25.470
come to pass. Virtually all of my mail was really mean and hate mail. But a lot of people were,

01:41:25.630 --> 01:41:29.940
I think, kind of puzzled, sort of like my dad. The way he heard

01:41:29.940 --> 01:41:29.960
Thank you.

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:36.830
the Kerner report was, Mr. Harris, out of the goodness of your heart, you ought to pay more

01:41:36.830 --> 01:41:44.870
taxes to help poor black people who are rioting in Detroit. He said this to me. He said, to hell

01:41:44.870 --> 01:41:50.770
with that. I'm having a hard enough time myself. I'm already paying too much tax and not getting

01:41:50.770 --> 01:41:57.260
anything for it. And that was true. A new white backlash is plainly visible in the country.

01:41:57.670 --> 01:41:59.850
The lead story in today's Wall Street Journal.

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:03.140
is headed, ghetto violence brings hardening of attitudes

01:42:03.140 --> 01:42:04.320
toward Negro gains.

01:42:04.800 --> 01:42:08.880
Law and order, broadly defined, becomes the most pressing,

01:42:09.300 --> 01:42:12.200
most powerful political issue for white voters

01:42:12.200 --> 01:42:14.140
in America by early 1968.

01:42:14.570 --> 01:42:18.300
Urban unrest is the number one concern.

01:42:18.540 --> 01:42:25.160
The sale of firearms has doubled in the last year.

01:42:25.990 --> 01:42:28.910
The tension of Negro disillusionment is in the air,

01:42:28.930 --> 01:42:29.970
and so is white.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:37.050
anxiety with both sides during an incident that will escalate why I'm

01:42:37.050 --> 01:42:39.830
buying because I won't be able to shoot through a car at somewhere if they

01:42:39.830 --> 01:42:46.230
bomb my house I'm going to be prepared I got food I got water right now I got

01:42:46.230 --> 01:42:58.960
about 150 rounds of shells my whole neighborhood's that way we're going to do

01:42:58.960 --> 01:42:59.660
in the year 1960

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:01.960
is going to determine the future of this country

01:43:01.960 --> 01:43:03.580
for decades and decades ahead.

01:43:07.080 --> 01:43:08.660
Very little in history is inevitable.

01:43:09.410 --> 01:43:11.110
It's important to remember because sometimes

01:43:11.110 --> 01:43:14.930
in our more cynical stage, we think nothing matters

01:43:14.930 --> 01:43:16.250
and it's all going to be bad.

01:43:16.510 --> 01:43:18.270
And actually, we don't know how it's going to matter,

01:43:18.350 --> 01:43:18.910
but it matters.

01:43:22.640 --> 01:43:25.300
I run because it is now unmistakably clear

01:43:25.300 --> 01:43:29.960
that we can change these disastrous, divisive...

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:37.280
policies only by changing the men who are now making them. But the reality of recent events

01:43:37.280 --> 01:43:45.620
in Vietnam has been glossed over with illusions. The report of the riot commission has been

01:43:45.620 --> 01:43:53.940
largely ignored. Crisis in our cities and in our ghettos have all been met with too little

01:43:53.940 --> 01:43:59.930
and too late. I think Robert Kennedy really cared.

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:05.260
about the country. And increasingly to him, that included marginalized people, including

01:44:05.260 --> 01:44:06.080
African-Americans.

01:44:19.030 --> 01:44:22.430
My thanks to all of you, and now it's on to Chicago, and let's win there.

01:45:01.120 --> 01:45:04.480
What I think about the future, there ain't no future, that's all it is to it.

01:45:04.500 --> 01:45:05.580
No future at all.

01:45:06.330 --> 01:45:07.730
Why don't you think it's your future?

01:45:08.520 --> 01:45:19.230
They killed Kennedy, they killed King, they killed Evers, they killed all the people that do something for the black men, you know, that stood up for them.

01:45:19.720 --> 01:45:29.660
I have to believe, maybe because I want to believe, that if Kennedy had lived, the Kerner Commission would have lived and the country would be in such a different place.

01:45:31.850 --> 01:45:36.440
But instead, Nixon comes along and says, we're not doing this.

01:45:36.540 --> 01:45:44.310
For the past five years, we have been deluged by government programs for the unemployed,

01:45:44.610 --> 01:45:46.870
programs for the cities, programs for the poor,

01:45:47.760 --> 01:45:54.280
and we have reaped from these programs an ugly harvest of frustration, violence, and failure across the land.

01:45:54.980 --> 01:45:59.960
I say it's time to quit pouring billions of dollars into programs that have failed in the United States.

01:46:00.060 --> 01:46:06.930
States of America. Richard Nixon repeats all the things that the conservatives were saying about

01:46:06.930 --> 01:46:12.380
the Kerner Commission report, that it seems to reward rioters, that there needs to be a greater

01:46:12.380 --> 01:46:17.260
emphasis on law and order. And that becomes the central theme of his campaign in 1968,

01:46:17.260 --> 01:46:22.410
was law and order. Let us recognize that the first civil right of every American

01:46:22.620 --> 01:46:29.880
is to be free from domestic violence. Richard Nixon will very skillfully tap

01:46:29.880 --> 01:46:29.960
I'll see you next time.

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:33.360
into this sense of what about the non-protesters?

01:46:37.140 --> 01:46:39.340
We've lost sight of the fact that many Americans

01:46:39.340 --> 01:46:41.300
still see America as a land of opportunity.

01:46:43.670 --> 01:46:47.380
Why all this focus on unhappy people,

01:46:48.000 --> 01:46:49.600
people demanding things,

01:46:50.200 --> 01:46:53.640
and worse, burning things down to further their agenda?

01:46:56.360 --> 01:46:58.400
This is not the American way.

01:47:05.080 --> 01:47:09.140
Lyndon Johnson had introduced a new punitive element

01:47:09.140 --> 01:47:10.680
in national policy.

01:47:11.300 --> 01:47:12.940
Of course, Johnson did this alongside

01:47:13.660 --> 01:47:15.960
his ambitious social welfare program.

01:47:17.410 --> 01:47:19.070
When Richard Nixon took office,

01:47:19.400 --> 01:47:21.560
he abandoned the social welfare part

01:47:21.560 --> 01:47:24.510
and really seized on the punitive elements

01:47:24.510 --> 01:47:26.570
of the Great Society and expanded them.

01:47:28.200 --> 01:47:29.980
I, Richard Billhaus Nixon...

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:31.440
and do solemnly swear.

01:47:31.700 --> 01:47:36.740
So we begin to see even more aggressive policing tactics

01:47:36.740 --> 01:47:39.160
being embraced by national policymakers.

01:47:39.240 --> 01:47:42.950
We begin to see more draconian laws

01:47:42.950 --> 01:47:45.310
and sentencing provisions being enacted,

01:47:45.310 --> 01:47:48.450
and the carceral state continued to expand.

01:47:50.800 --> 01:47:54.340
The disturbances, unfortunately, had a short-term effect

01:47:54.340 --> 01:47:56.000
if you're going to demagogue about it,

01:47:56.080 --> 01:47:59.970
and was very helpful to Nixon and to other conservatives.

01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:06.620
politicians since then we began to turn backwards on desegregation and on

01:48:06.620 --> 01:48:16.150
virtually everything else there was a long period of social conservatism after

01:48:16.150 --> 01:48:22.590
the riots prompted by the riots they issued in a racially conservative

01:48:22.590 --> 01:48:29.660
political era in which law and order and repression pretty much

01:48:30.160 --> 01:48:34.520
prevailed, and that suited most of the white citizenry

01:48:34.520 --> 01:48:35.080
of the country.

01:48:41.860 --> 01:48:43.460
I've talked to a lot of people about this

01:48:43.460 --> 01:48:47.220
and consistently heard people my age, early 50,

01:48:47.420 --> 01:48:50.450
say, you know, I grew up in the suburbs of Detroit.

01:48:50.490 --> 01:48:52.630
My parents always said, you can't go to Detroit.

01:48:52.630 --> 01:48:55.550
It's not safe, which was just code word for saying

01:48:55.550 --> 01:48:56.430
black people live there.

01:49:00.090 --> 01:49:01.990
The ghetto was reinforced.

01:49:07.130 --> 01:49:12.340
The commission told the truth, but it was a harsh truth.

01:49:15.060 --> 01:49:20.700
Secretary of Labor back then said you can capsulize the commission's report

01:49:20.700 --> 01:49:24.540
in the words of that great American philosopher Pogo,

01:49:25.590 --> 01:49:28.730
who said, we have met the enemy and he is us.

01:49:45.580 --> 01:49:51.970
service on the Kerner Commission and the advocacy which followed it in a way kind

01:49:51.970 --> 01:49:59.870
of ruined me as a senator. Hubert Humphrey was vice president when I became a senator at age 33.

01:50:00.000 --> 01:50:07.980
and he said that compromise is the essence of the operations of a legislative body.

01:50:09.010 --> 01:50:15.420
He said, I don't mean compromise on principle, but I mean on how we do it or how much we do this year.

01:50:19.280 --> 01:50:25.300
And I came to a time after the Kerner report that I just...

01:50:30.000 --> 01:50:38.330
I couldn't compromise. I knew that that's what we'd been doing, and that had got us in the mess we were in.

01:50:47.480 --> 01:50:59.040
We had people around the Senate even who said, well, black people in America are much better off than they are anywhere else in the world, you know, in Zambia or somewhere.

01:51:00.320 --> 01:51:03.080
The answer to that is, these are Americans.

01:51:03.700 --> 01:51:08.120
They're not in Africa, and they're not trying to tear down the system.

01:51:08.320 --> 01:51:10.280
They want to be a part of it.

01:51:11.860 --> 01:51:17.270
They're trying to get the country to do what it says it believes in.

01:51:18.360 --> 01:51:20.900
They try to make democracy work.

01:51:23.600 --> 01:51:28.610
My first serious engagement with the Kerner Report was in 1992.

01:51:28.610 --> 01:51:29.960
in the afternoon.

01:51:30.000 --> 01:51:32.860
of the Rodney King uprisings in Los Angeles.

01:51:33.610 --> 01:51:39.180
And you heard the word Kerner again and again and again.

01:51:40.540 --> 01:51:43.770
And it has remained this touchstone,

01:51:44.730 --> 01:51:46.630
you know, this vitally important document

01:51:46.940 --> 01:51:50.770
that unfortunately has retained its relevance

01:51:50.770 --> 01:51:52.680
over the span of history.

01:51:59.230 --> 01:51:59.970
The Kerner Commission...

01:52:00.000 --> 01:52:02.880
did not deign to take the easy way out.

01:52:03.228 --> 01:52:04.988
they told the nation.

01:52:05.628 --> 01:52:07.628
what exactly the problem was.

01:52:08.348 --> 01:52:10.508
even if it meant that no one was going to listen to them.

01:52:31.308 --> 01:52:37.388
American Experience, The Riot Report is available with PBS Passport and on Amazon Prime Video.
